{:edb/db-before-t 1556 :edb/db-after-t 1557 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534869 50 #inst "2026-10-08T09:36:38.965Z" 13194139534869 true] [17592186054494 1100 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty { {{n}}(x-1)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=1$ of $f'(x).$" 13194139534869 true] [17592186054494 1100 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n$\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty {{{n}}(x-1)^{n}}$.\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=1$ of $f'(x)$." 13194139534869 false] [17592186054494 1145 "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty { {{n}}(x-1)^{n}} = (x-1) + {2(x-1)^2} + {3(x-1)^3} +\\cdots .\n$$\n\nTo find $f'(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1+0+4(x-1)+9(x-1)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+4(x-1)+9(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534869 true] [17592186054494 1145 "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty {{{n}}(x-1)^{n}} = (x-1) + {2(x-1)^2} + {3(x-1)^3} +\\cdots\n$$\n\nTo find $f'(x)$, we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= 1 + 0 + 4(x - 1) + 9(x - 1)^{2} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1 + 4(x - 1) + 9(x - 1)^{2} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534869 false] [17592186054495 1100 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{ (x-3)^{n}}{n}\n$$\n\nFind $a_n,$ given that\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n.\n$$" 13194139534869 true] [17592186054495 1100 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is given by\n$\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(x-3)^{n}}{n}$\nFind $a_n$, given that\n\n$f'(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$." 13194139534869 false] [17592186054495 1145 "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/assets/7316ce9632bc2ab0347accb71c4c0dc90db8a87e0f9bead704e0218523a9b367.png)\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/assets/846a66292603d3436219826be2e9419b5798fa121341d1e1676fba63c0f37eec.png)\nWe can now write the Taylor series for $f'(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (x-3)^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\na_n = (x-3)^{n-1}.\n$$" 13194139534869 true] [17592186054495 1145 "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nn\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$n$\nWe can now write the Taylor series for $f'(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (x-3)^{n-1}\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\na_n = (x-3)^{n-1}\n$$" 13194139534869 false] [17592186054496 1100 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$  about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-3)^{n}\\,x^n}{n^3}\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x| <            R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" 13194139534869 true] [17592186054496 1100 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n$\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-3)^{n}\\,x^n}{n^3}$\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $\\mid x \\mid < R$, where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, about $x=0$." 13194139534869 false] [17592186054496 1145 "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of $f'(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x)$. So, to find the radius of convergence of $f'(x)$, we need to compute $R$.\n\nTo calculate $R$, we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid \\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \\mid \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid ((-3)^{n + 1}x^{n + 1})/((n + 1)^{3}) \\cdot (n^{3})/((-3)^{n}x^{n}) \\mid \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid ((-3)x \\cdot n^{3})/((n + 1)^{3}) \\mid \\\\\n&= 3\\begin{vmatrix}x & lim_(n → ∞)(1)/((1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{3}) \\\\ = 3 & x\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges when $L<1$. So, it converges when\n\n$$\n3 \\mid x \\mid \\mid < 1; \\mid x \\mid \\mid < \\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is\n\n$$\nR = \\dfrac 1 3\n$$" 13194139534869 false] [17592186054496 1145 "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of $f'(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of $f'(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-3)^{n+1}\\,{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{3}}{(-3)^{n}\\,{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-3)x\\cdot {n}^{3}}{(n+1)^{3}}| \\\\  & =3|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{3}} \\\\  & =3|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|x| & <1 \\\\ |x| & <\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R = \\dfrac 1 3.$" 13194139534869 true] [17592186054595 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316149 13194139534869 true] [17592186054595 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316177 13194139534869 true] [17592186054595 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316184 13194139534869 true] [17592186054595 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316188 13194139534869 true] [17592186054595 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316206 13194139534869 true] [17592186054596 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316195 13194139534869 true] [17592186054596 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316199 13194139534869 true] [17592186054596 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316213 13194139534869 true] [17592186054596 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316227 13194139534869 true] [17592186054596 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316234 13194139534869 true] [17592186054597 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316156 13194139534869 true] [17592186054597 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316163 13194139534869 true] [17592186054597 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316170 13194139534869 true] [17592186054597 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316202 13194139534869 true] [17592186054597 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316220 13194139534869 true] [17592186316149 1017 #edb/ref 17592186316150 13194139534869 true] [17592186316149 1089 "q-126101" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316149 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534869 true] [17592186316149 1100 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{5n-1}(x-1)^{n}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=1$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316149 1113 #uuid "4b94c18d-8d1b-52c1-b39b-7b21a15a8977" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316149 1145 "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{5n-1}(x-1)^{n}=(x-1)+\\frac{4}{9}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{2}{7}(x-1)^{3}+⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1+\\frac{8}{9}(x-1)+\\frac{6}{7}(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\n$$" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316150 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316151 13194139534869 true] [17592186316150 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316152 13194139534869 true] [17592186316150 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316153 13194139534869 true] [17592186316150 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316154 13194139534869 true] [17592186316150 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316155 13194139534869 true] [17592186316150 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534869 true] [17592186316150 1041 "selection" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316150 1104 #edb/ref 17592186316152 13194139534869 true] [17592186316150 1110 #uuid "498bacbd-078c-59c4-826e-caf996bc2d85" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316151 1034 "{f}^{′}(x)=1+\\frac{4}{9}(x-1)+\\frac{3}{7}(x-1)^{2}+⋯" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316151 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534869 true] [17592186316151 1111 #uuid "2b1ff20f-c375-52a8-ac88-0679a78f9485" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316152 1034 "{f}^{′}(x)=1+\\frac{8}{9}(x-1)+\\frac{6}{7}(x-1)^{2}+⋯" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316152 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534869 true] [17592186316152 1111 #uuid "7e51aaa7-d3fc-531e-aaea-4b31028cc64c" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316153 1034 "{f}^{′}(x)=1+\\frac{8}{3}(x-1)+\\frac{6}{5}(x-1)^{2}+⋯" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316153 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534869 true] [17592186316153 1111 #uuid "8e69d0fb-cfce-5f75-8cc1-b3cacce4e286" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316154 1034 "{f}^{′}(x)=1+\\frac{4}{9}(x-1)+\\frac{16}{81}(x-1)^{2}+⋯" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316154 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534869 true] [17592186316154 1111 #uuid "47dd878e-b75e-5088-a612-e742524e5c9c" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316155 1034 "{f}^{′}(x)=1+\\frac{8}{9}(x-1)+\\frac{16}{27}(x-1)^{2}+⋯" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316155 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534869 true] [17592186316155 1111 #uuid "da492dc5-edd3-5d82-a008-45a6ad5e601b" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316156 1017 #edb/ref 17592186316157 13194139534869 true] [17592186316156 1089 "q-126166" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316156 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534869 true] [17592186316156 1100 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2n}}{{2}^{2n}(n!)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316156 1113 #uuid "153b6d05-924f-5553-b86b-f60ba5f8d6c3" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316156 1145 "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{2n+2}}{{2}^{2n+2}((n+1)!)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{2n}(n!)^{2}}{{x}^{2n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}(n+1)^{2}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ Since $0<1$ for all $x,$ we conclude that $R=\\infty .$" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316157 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316158 13194139534869 true] [17592186316157 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316159 13194139534869 true] [17592186316157 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316160 13194139534869 true] [17592186316157 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316161 13194139534869 true] [17592186316157 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316162 13194139534869 true] [17592186316157 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534869 true] [17592186316157 1041 "selection" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316157 1104 #edb/ref 17592186316158 13194139534869 true] [17592186316157 1110 #uuid "29d9cb37-fb69-5132-bb14-d115532077ab" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316158 1034 "\\infty " 13194139534869 true] [17592186316158 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534869 true] [17592186316158 1111 #uuid "abd8e53d-ad49-505c-9a7f-4caa912fafa7" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316159 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316159 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534869 true] [17592186316159 1111 #uuid "59b3eaf0-8d1c-5156-a164-d2bd746c86f5" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316160 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316160 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534869 true] [17592186316160 1111 #uuid "c2df9d36-2e4d-54f3-8549-ab2e239fe8a1" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316161 1034 "4" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316161 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534869 true] [17592186316161 1111 #uuid "8e1513bf-9e46-5c8d-b61a-97a5c856e212" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316162 1034 "0" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316162 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534869 true] [17592186316162 1111 #uuid "aaf86e81-d76c-54ee-8587-12564a2b8205" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316163 1017 #edb/ref 17592186316164 13194139534869 true] [17592186316163 1089 "q-126168" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316163 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534869 true] [17592186316163 1100 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{2}^{n}n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316163 1113 #uuid "621b9ccf-4fef-5845-a96d-7e9c92f431e5" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316163 1145 "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{2}^{n+1}(n+1)}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{n}n}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{xn}{2(n+1)}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(1+\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|x|}{2} & <1 \\\\ |x| & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R=2.$" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316165 13194139534869 true] [17592186316164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316166 13194139534869 true] [17592186316164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316167 13194139534869 true] [17592186316164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316168 13194139534869 true] [17592186316164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316169 13194139534869 true] [17592186316164 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534869 true] [17592186316164 1041 "selection" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316164 1104 #edb/ref 17592186316167 13194139534869 true] [17592186316164 1110 #uuid "0691ab1c-aefd-59b7-a003-529f946303c8" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316165 1034 "\\infty " 13194139534869 true] [17592186316165 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534869 true] [17592186316165 1111 #uuid "b5fe0b1b-6daa-504f-8b6e-5832932e8ce5" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316166 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316166 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534869 true] [17592186316166 1111 #uuid "bc89ab29-05f0-5c86-a374-1266a83a8c19" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316167 1034 "2" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316167 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534869 true] [17592186316167 1111 #uuid "2d0693a7-f0d7-53a6-905e-bddd4a05b56d" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316168 1034 "1" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316168 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534869 true] [17592186316168 1111 #uuid "02819df1-9f6b-5691-8459-3d7d9dfd8fbe" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316169 1034 "0" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316169 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534869 true] [17592186316169 1111 #uuid "802bd5c6-7d12-5a18-8504-c93612e2722c" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316170 1017 #edb/ref 17592186316171 13194139534869 true] [17592186316170 1089 "q-126169" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316170 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534869 true] [17592186316170 1100 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\sqrt{n}\\,{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316170 1113 #uuid "285f64d1-e80a-5824-a8d3-10c0a11a59c6" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316170 1145 "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\sqrt{n+1}\\,{x}^{n+1}}{\\sqrt{n}\\,{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|x\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{n}}| \\\\  & =|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R=1.$" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316171 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316172 13194139534869 true] [17592186316171 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316173 13194139534869 true] [17592186316171 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316174 13194139534869 true] [17592186316171 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316175 13194139534869 true] [17592186316171 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316176 13194139534869 true] [17592186316171 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534869 true] [17592186316171 1041 "selection" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316171 1104 #edb/ref 17592186316175 13194139534869 true] [17592186316171 1110 #uuid "5ed3b3b4-e0cb-5bf2-a643-7ad5f17dc132" 13194139534869 true] [17592186316172 